  "final_text": "All four steps are done, and the rule reads back as enabled.\n\n**1. Data view 'Kibana Sample Data Logs'** (id `90943e30-9a47-11e8-b64d-95841ca0b247`, index `kibana_sample_data_logs`)\n- Time field: `timestamp`\n- Main fields: `@timestamp`, `timestamp`, `response` (string, with `response.keyword`), `request`, `url`, `clientip` (ip), `bytes` (long), `agent`, `host`, `machine.os`, `geo.*`, `tags`, `extension`, `referer`, and `utc_time`, among others.\n\n**2. Dashboard 'S4-a7d7e8c6 Web logs'** (id `28ceddaa-33c1-493e-ab96-0159c10d3884`)\n- Panel 1: line chart of count over time, date histogram on `timestamp`.\n- Panel 2: pie chart of count grouped by `response.keyword`.\n- I used a pie for the response-code panel. The API has no simple bar-by-terms shape without more setup.\n\n**3. Alert rule 'S4-a7d7e8c6 5xx errors'** (id `e777f94f-27c6-4745-b5f7-8e7e1de3d1f9`)\n- Type `.es-query`, consumer `alerts`, schedule every 1m, enabled at creation.\n- Query: count of documents with `response.keyword` in `\"500\"`..`\"599\"` over a 5-minute window on `timestamp`, threshold `> 10`.\n- `response` is a string, so the range is lexicographic. That matches numeric 500\u2013599 for three-digit codes. I couldn't check the distinct response values directly, because Elasticsearch isn't reachable from this sandbox and the console proxy is disabled.\n- No actions are attached.\n\n**4. Panel rename and read-back**\n- The pie panel is now titled 'Responses by code'. The dashboard still has exactly two panels.\n- Reading the rule back shows `enabled: true`, `schedule: 1m`, and an execution status of `ok`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
